                                lloeki on June 8, 2012 [–]

                                You should strive to not rely on type-checking, and trust and rely
                                more on duck-typing. If at some point you need to ensure something
                                is not None, test it and act accordingly in the alternate case. If
                                something absolutely must not be None (or must have a specific
                                method), ever, use assert() so that things blow up upfront where you
                                can infer (or even read) the reason, and not deeper in the code
                                where the actual error message, as you mention, has to be parsed
                                according to the whole call stack to make sense of it.

                                Sometimes you may need to type-dispatch when you want to do smart
                                functions. A typical example is a function that takes a
                                sequence/iterable/generator of strings or a string. Since a string
                                is iterable in python, you have no choice but to check for
                                BaseString.



